# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Line endings, per file type. This file exists because of one real failure.
#
# `deploy-android.bat` was written with LF-only endings — by a tool, not by a
# checkout — and cmd.exe cannot run such a file. It does not report a syntax
# error either: it reads a batch file by BYTE OFFSET, so LF-only endings leave
# it a byte behind on every line and it executes the tail ends of lines as if
# they were commands. What that looks like from the outside is:
#
#     'ncy' is not recognized as an internal or external command
#     'loy-android.bat' is not recognized as an internal or external command
#     'cho' is not recognized as an internal or external command
#
# — fragments of "fancy", "deploy-android.bat" and "echo". Nothing in that
# output points at line endings, which is what makes it worth writing down.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is `true` on this machine, so a fresh CHECKOUT would have been
# fine. It only bites files written straight to the working tree, and it will
# bite again the next time one is. These attributes make the answer part of the
# repository instead of part of one machine's config.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Read by cmd.exe, which needs CRLF. Never let autocrlf or a tool decide.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The mirror-image failure: these run on the Ubuntu build/deploy VPS, and a CR
# at the end of a line reaches bash as part of the command. `deployment/
# redeploy.sh` would fail with `$'\r': command not found` on every single line.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Gradle's own wrapper scripts, same split, same reasons.
gradlew text eol=lf
gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
